```{r}
b0 <- m$coefficients[1] %>% as.numeric() %>% round(2)
b1 <- m$coefficients[2] %>% as.numeric() %>% round(2)
b2 <- m$coefficients[3] %>% as.numeric() %>% round(2)
b3 <- m$coefficients[4] %>% as.numeric() %>% round(2)

b0                 # C1
b0 + b1            # T1
b0 + b2            # C2
b0 + b1 + b2 + b3  # T2
b0 + b1 + b2       # CF

CF <- b0 + b1 + b2
T2 <- b0 + b1 + b2 + b3   
T2-CF
```


## Question 4a

> Are the treatment and control groups equivalent prior to the intervention? How do you know?

Yes. treat.dummy=-0.07 and is not significant, so treatment and control groups don't differ meaningfully prior to the intervention. 

## Question 4b

> Do we observe secular trends (gains independent of the treatment)? How do you know?

Yes. post.dummy=1.28 and is significant, p<0.01. The control group experience meaningful gains over time even without treatment, confirming a secular trend.

## Question 4c

> What is the effect size in this model (gains from the treatment)?

The effect size is b3 = treat.post.dummy = 1.63


## Question 4d

> What does statistical significance of b3 represent? In other words, which contrast is being tested?

Statistical significance of b3 represents whether teh treatment group's gain over time differs from the control group's gain, or trend, over the same time period. It's ultilately testing the actual treatment effect net of the underlying secular trend, comparing (T2-T1) To (C2-C1). 


