## Question 5a

> Do the reflexive and diff-in-diff models generate the same results (approximately)?  Why?

No, reflexive results to 2.91, DiD results to 1.63. They differ because the reflexive model doeszn't remove the secular trend, it attributes the whole pre-post gain in teh treatment group to treatment, when about 1.28 of it reflects the trent that would have happened anyway. 

## Question 5b

> Do the post-test only and diff-in-diff models generate the same results (approximately)? Why?

Yes, post-test only give 1.55 and DiD gives 1.63. This makes sense because the baseline equivalance assumption is held, treat.dummy was not significant in T1. When groups start out equal, a simple post test comparison and the DiD estimator converge on similar answers. 



