## Question 6a

> Are the pre-treatment differences (C1=T1?) different in this model versus the previous diff-in-diff? Why or why not?

Yes. treat.dummy=-2.16 and is highly significant, versus -0.07 and nonsignificant in the treatment versus control model. The high-SES group starts at a muhc higher baseline ability level than the treatment group, while the control group did not. 



## Question 6b

> Does the diff-in-diff model require that study groups are equivalent prior to treatment to generate valid results?

No, DiD does not require groups to be equivalent at baseline, the only requirement is without treatment, they would have parallel trends. Differences in starting level as fine as long as the rate of change over time would be similar. 


## Question 6c

> Is the secular trend identified by this model different from the previous diff-in-diff (approximately)? Why or why not?

They are roughly similar in concept, but not identical in magnitude. The trend of post.dummy=1.71, is larger than in the control-based model of 1.28. Both indicate a secular trend, though the estimated size differs somewhat depending on which comparison group is used. 



## Question 6d

> The treatment effects from this model are approximately the same as the previous diff-in-diff model, even though they use very different comparison groups. Why does this model still work using the high SES group?

Since DiD relies on the comparison group tracking the trend the treatment group would have experienced without treatment, not the level. Even though high-SES group starts much higher, its rate of change over time from T1-T2 approximates what the treatment groups trend would have ben absent over time. That's the reason for the treatment effect estimate of 1.20 being in the same realm of the control based DiD estimate of 1.63, despite teh very different baseline levels. 



## Question 6e

> What is the identification assumption of the diff-in-diff model?

The identifying assumption of DiD is the parallel trends assumption: absent treatment, the treatment and compairson groups would have experienced the same trend over time, and not that they start at the same level. 


